  "label": "Columbia",
  "style_primary": "cool-jazz",
  "rationale": "The third and widely-considered finest collaboration between Miles Davis and Gil Evans, recorded July 22 and 29 and August 4 and 18, 1958 at Columbia's 30th Street Studio and released March 1959 on Columbia (CL 1274/CS 8085). A full-orchestra reading of George Gershwin's opera Porgy and Bess with Evans's arrangements (plus one Evans original, 'Gone'), carrying Davis's modal approach into a large-ensemble setting — Davis told Nat Hentoff that for 'I Loves You, Porgy' Evans 'only wrote a scale for me. No chords.' Consensus is the strongest signal here: the Penguin Guide to Jazz (Cook 2002, p.376), AllMusic (Lindsay Planer review), Rolling Stone (2004, p.214-217), The Encyclopedia of Popular Music, and the New York Times (John S. Wilson, 1959) all rate it among Davis's and Evans's best work, and it sits in the same canon tier as Miles Ahead and Sketches of Spain, both already in the canon. It is also a defining statement of orchestral/cool jazz and third-stream, and a load-bearing link in the modal lineage that leads to Kind of Blue (1959), recorded months later with much of the same rhythm section (Paul Chambers, Jimmy Cobb). Session gravity is exceptional: the orchestra includes Gunther Schuller, Julius Watkins, and Willie Ruff on French horn, plus Cannonball Adderley, Paul Chambers, Philly Joe Jones, and Jimmy Cobb.",
  "priority": "must_have",
  "epistemic": "obs",
